{"reply":"No—the contrast doesn’t by itself mean your confident work-self is an act or your quieter family-self is the “real” one. It may show one person adapting their voice to different relationships while remaining connected by values, judgments, and concerns. Your earlier meeting-and-dinner note seems especially important here: you held the same view in both settings and felt comfortable in both; what unsettled you was noticing the difference afterward.

That makes your experience closer to a question about continuity than a test for which self is authentic. Hume argues that we do not encounter one unchanging inner self, but a succession of perceptions and experiences that we connect into a person over time ([*Of personal identity*](https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6)). Alice dramatizes the anxiety of that problem when the Caterpillar asks, “Who are _you?_” and she struggles to explain herself after feeling changed ([Chapter 5](https://www.gutenberg.org/files/11/11-h/11-h.htm)). But the chapter does not establish that one social voice is false; it gives us the disorientation of not knowing how to describe a changing self.

